WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:03.070
The Baťa Skyscraper,
in Zlín, Czechia,

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.580
is a landmark of
architecture.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.960
I know it doesn't look like much
by modern standards,

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.700
but this isn't from the
1970s or 1980s...

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.920
it was completed in 1939.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.769
It was Administration Building
Number 21,

00:00:15.769 --> 00:00:17.529
built to run the Baťa company,

00:00:17.529 --> 00:00:19.340
which had started as a
small shoe manufacturer

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:22.130
and grown into a
huge multinational corporation

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:26.070
across dozens of different industries
with tens of thousands of employees.

00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.300
And this building wasn't
just modern in style.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.380
For a pre-World War 2 building,
it's surprisingly advanced.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:34.370
It has air conditioning and heating,
adjustable for each floor.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.100
It has a couple of small
paternoster lifts

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.780
for getting between
adjacent floors,

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.100
a continuous chain of cabins
that never stop:

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:42.460
they're very efficient,
just a bit dangerous.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.750
And the boss has a
corner office on every floor.

00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:49.379
Because the boss's office...
is an elevator.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:51.480
The control panel is
next to the desk,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:52.930
and we're going up.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.539
However, only the
museum staff here

00:00:55.539 --> 00:00:57.520
are actually allowed
to push the buttons,

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.000
so please this enjoy this
little bit of camera trickery.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.030
The folks who are showing me around
didn't want to be on camera,

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:09.030
which is fair enough!

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.740
So I'm going to be
your tour guide today.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:13.880
This elevator was built by Otis,
the American elevator company,

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.450
for Jan Antonín Baťa,
the head of the company.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:17.640
And the technical side of this

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.280
is roughly the same as
any other large goods lift:

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.570
Otis have built much bigger and
heavier elevators than this.

00:01:23.570 --> 00:01:27.270
Scaling up to five metres by
five metres isn't too difficult,

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:29.420
and the furnishings don't
add that much weight.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.500
It does have a few
extra features:

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.700
two telephones, one for local calls
and one for international;

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.680
plus separate lighting and
air conditioning, but those were

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.860
pretty much all solved problems
for elevators by 1939.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.670
If you're running
one cable for lighting,

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:43.600
it's not much more effort
to add a few more

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.899
to power some fans and
provide telephone lines.

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:49.220
But the bit that surprised me
was the sink.

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:53.170
How is there hot and cold
running water, and a drain,

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:54.360
in a moving elevator?

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:55.740
When I first heard about this,

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.210
I thought there'd be a sink
installed on each floor

00:01:58.210 --> 00:01:59.240
and it would just line up,

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.409
but no, this moves
with the elevator.

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:04.340
And then I thought maybe there was
some incredibly clever plumbing,

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.820
like in that rotating house
I visited a while ago...

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:09.530
but actually there's just
a water tank above

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:11.930
and a wastewater tank below
and every so often

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.310
maintenance would fill that one
and empty that one

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.400
because sometimes the simplest
solutions are the best.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.940
These days, it's kept dry.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:22.920
Now, there is a story that
the boss would use this elevator

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.290
to always keep a watch on employees,
appearing at any time,

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.900
staring from his chair
out of the door,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:30.920
but the guides here say
that's a myth.

00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:32.610
And it doesn't
make sense anyway.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:36.310
Because each floor of this building
is 80 metres by 20 metres,

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.750
and this is a corner office
with fairly narrow doors.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:42.010
There just aren't the sightlines
to make that possible.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:45.430
And besides, there's a big
light-up indicator outside

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.170
that tells you what floor
the office is currently sitting on.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.680
It's not recorded whether employees
would ever just push the button

00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:52.430
to call the office to their
floor as a prank,

00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:55.100
I really hope that happened sometimes.

00:02:55.100 --> 00:02:58.640
I couldn't tell you for sure
why this was built.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:00.330
It's probably efficiency?

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:02.920
It is quite a long walk to
the main public elevators,

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.410
plus it's much more private,

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:07.360
rather than the boss riding along
with everyone else.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.920
And besides: Jan Antonín Baťa
never actually got to use this.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.330
Because the building was
completed in 1939,

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.250
by which point Europe
was at war,

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:19.500
and Jan Antonín had left the
continent with his family.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:21.420
The history of him, and the
fates of the various

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.790
international branches of the
company after that are...

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:25.790
complicated.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.710
Complicated enough that you could probably
get a whole PhD trying to understand it.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:33.760
After the war, he was declared a traitor,
and he died in exile in Brazil in 1965.

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.120
But in 2007, the conviction was
officially overturned,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.970
and in 2019, he was given Czechia's
highest honor for services to the country,

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:45.670
and there's a statue of him
in the park just over the road.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:49.220
History is complicated, particularly
Second World War history in central Europe,

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.170
and particularly when
I just came here

00:03:51.170 --> 00:03:53.750
to make a video about
an interesting elevator.

00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:57.480
This building is now the main
government office for this whole region,

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:59.500
and a local tourist attraction.

00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.060
And every so often,
someone still stops by

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:04.047
to service the elevator.

